ed by the utility that agents actually achieve, neither theory dominates the other across well-known decision dilemmas (Gibbard and Harper 1978; Ahmed 2014). In Newcomb’s problem, where a reliable predictor fills an opaque box with $\\$1,000,000$ if and only if she predicts the agent will leave behind a transparent box containing $\\$1,000$, EDT one-boxes and walks away rich, whereas CDT two-boxes and walks away with $\\$1,000$. In Skyrms’s smoking lesion problem (Skyrms 1980), where a pre-existing arterial lesion causes both a desire to smoke and fatal lung cancer, CDT recognizes that smoking does not cause cancer and enjoys the cigar, whereas EDT refrains from smoking to \"manage the news\" (Lewis 1981). Worse still, in sequential prediction dilemmas such as Parfit’s hitchhiker problem (Parfit 1986) and Drescher’s transparent Newcomb problem (Drescher 2006), both CDT and EDT fail to secure the high-payoff outcome.\n\nIn *\"Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality\"* (2017), Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares propose **functional decision theory (FDT)** to resolve this impasse. The philosophical core of the functional approach is to shift the locus of evaluation from a physical action token to a mathematical function type. An agent implementing a decision algorithm is instantiating a mathematical function. If a twin in a separate room, or a predictor’s simulation of the agent, instantiates or models that same mathematical function, then the agent’s physical action and the twin’s or predictor’s behavior are bound together by **subjunctive dependence**:\n\n> *\"Functional decision theory is to subjunctive dependence as causal decision theory is to causal dependence.\"* (Yudkowsky and Soares 2017, Sec. 3)\n\nHowever, within Yudkowsky and Soares’s paper, there are actually two distinct formalizations of the functional decision rule. In the main text, for pedagogical simplicity, the authors formalize what we may call **action-based FDT**, in which the agent intervenes on an observation-indexed node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x})$ to choose a single action $a \\in \\mathcal{A}$. Yet in Footnote 7 and Footnote 16, the authors explicitly note that this action-level formula is a simplification of their preferred theory, which we will call **policy functional decision theory (P-FDT)**:\n\n> *\"In the authors' preferred formalization of FDT, agents actually iterate over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions. This makes a difference in certain multi-agent dilemmas, but will not make a difference in this paper.\"* (Footnote 7)\n\nAnd again, when discussing how functional agents respond to observations in Section 9:\n\n> *\"As mentioned earlier, the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node $\\mathrm{FDT}(-)$ to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action. The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell, so we have set that distinction aside in this paper for ease of exposition.\"* (Footnote 16)\n\nBecause the reference paper sets **P-FDT** aside after these brief remarks, a reader who wants to apply P-FDT to concrete problems must reconstruct its exact mathematical machinery, see how it handles single-agent and multi-agent counterfactuals, and resolve the technical questions that Footnote 16 leaves open. That is the goal of this essay.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Formal Architecture of P-FDT\n\n### 2.1 Decision-Theoretic Primitives\n\nFollowing the formal framework of Joyce (1999) and Yudkowsky and Soares (2017, Sec. 5), consider an agent facing a decision problem defined over:\n* A countable set of possible outcomes $\\mathcal{O} = \\{o_1, o_2, \\dots\\}$.\n* A bounded real-valued utility function $\\mathcal{U}: \\mathcal{O} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, inducing the random variable $\\textsc{V} \\coloneqq \\mathcal{U}(\\textsc{Outcome})$.\n* A countable set of possible observation histories $\\mathcal{X}$.\n* A finite set of available physical actions $\\mathcal{A}$.\n\nAny expected utility theory evaluates options by computing the expectation of $\\textsc{V}$ inside a hypothetical model constructed from the agent’s epistemic state $M$. Where the theories diverge is in how they represent $M$ and how they construct hypotheticals from $M$ upon receiving an observation $x \\in \\mathcal{X}$:\n\n1. **Evidential Decision Theory (EDT)** represents $M$ as a probability distribution $P$ and constructs hypotheticals by conditioning on both the observation $\\textsc{Obs} = x$ and the physical act $\\textsc{Act} = a$:\n   $$\\mathrm{EDT}(P, x) \\coloneqq \\operatorname*{argmax}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}} \\mathbb{E}\\left(\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\textsc{Obs} = x, \\textsc{Act} = a\\right).$$\n\n2. **Causal Decision Theory (CDT)** augments $P$ with a directed acyclic causal graph $G$, using Pearl’s (1996, 2000) intervention operator $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{Act} = a)$ to sever incoming causal arrows into $\\textsc{Act}$ while conditioning on $\\textsc{Obs} = x$:\n   $$\\mathrm{CDT}(P, G, x) \\coloneqq \\operatorname*{argmax}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}} \\mathbb{E}\\left(\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{Act} = a), \\textsc{Obs} = x\\right).$$\n\n3. **Action-Based FDT** (Equation 4 of Yudkowsky and Soares) augments $P$ with an extended dependency graph $G$ that encodes not only physical causation, but also logical, mathematical, and computational dependencies. Instead of conditioning on $\\textsc{Obs} = x$ and intervening on $\\textsc{Act}$, action-based FDT leaves $P$ unconditioned and uses the observation $x$ to select which logical node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x})$ to intervene upon:\n   $$\\mathrm{FDT}(P, G, x) \\coloneqq \\operatorname*{argmax}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}} \\mathbb{E}\\left(\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}\\left(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x}) = a\\right)\\right),$$\n   where underlines denote dequotation (converting values into syntactic variable names via Kleene’s 1938 second recursion theorem).\n\n### 2.2 Defining Policies and the P-FDT Decision Rule\n\nTo formalize **P-FDT** from Footnotes 7 and 16, we introduce the space of deterministic policies $\\Pi$. Taking observations $x \\in \\mathcal{X}$ as the runtime inputs that vary across branches of a decision problem, a policy is a function mapping observations to actions:\n$$\\Pi \\coloneqq \\mathcal{A}^{\\mathcal{X}} = \\{\\pi \\mid \\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}\\}.$$\n(When $\\mathcal{X}$ and $\\mathcal{A}$ are finite, $\\Pi$ is a finite set of cardinality $|\\mathcal{A}|^{|\\mathcal{X}|}$.)\n\nIn a P-FDT world-model $M = (P, G)$, the graph $G$ contains a single logical node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ taking values in $\\Pi$. This node represents the mathematical policy output by the P-FDT algorithm when given the world-model $(P, G)$. Downstream of $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ in $G$ are:\n1. The agent’s physical action node $\\textsc{Act}$, which is determined by applying the policy to the realized observation $\\textsc{Obs}$:\n   $$\\textsc{Act}(\\pi, x) \\coloneqq \\pi(x).$$\n2. Any environmental variables—such as a predictor’s prediction $\\textsc{Prediction}$ or a psychological twin’s action $\\textsc{TwinAct}$—that subjunctively depend on the agent’s decision algorithm. Crucially, if a predictor simulates how the agent will respond to a hypothetical observation $x' \\in \\mathcal{X}$, the predictor’s node depends on $\\pi(x')$, which is simply the evaluation of $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ at $x'$.\n\nFollowing Footnote 16 literally—the algorithm *\"intervenes on the node $\\mathrm{FDT}(-)$ to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action\"*—**P-FDT** is a two-stage procedure:\n\n**Stage 1 (Global Policy Selection):** The agent identifies the optimal policy $\\pi^*_{(P, G)} \\in \\Pi$ by intervening on the policy node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ and maximizing unconditional expected utility:\n$$\\pi^*_{(P, G)} \\coloneqq \\operatorname*{argmax}_{\\pi \\in \\Pi} \\mathbb{E}\\left(\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}\\left(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi\\right)\\right). \\tag{P-FDT.1}$$\n\n**Stage 2 (Local Policy Application):** Given her actual observation $x \\in \\mathcal{X}$, the agent evaluates the optimal policy at $x$ to select her physical action:\n$$\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, x) \\coloneqq \\pi^*_{(P, G)}(x). \\tag{P-FDT.2}$$\n\nCombining both stages into a single expression yields the complete **P-FDT** equation:\n$$\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, x) = \\left[\\operatorname*{argmax}_{\\pi \\in \\mathcal{A}^{\\mathcal{X}}} \\mathbb{E}\\left(\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}\\left(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi\\right)\\right)\\right](x). \\tag{P-FDT}$$\n\n### 2.3 Counterpossibles and Subjunctive Surgery in P-FDT\n\nWhy does P-FDT use Pearl’s $\\mathtt{do}$ operator on a logical variable $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$? Mathematically, $\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, -)$ is a deterministic function; on fixed inputs $(P, G)$, it has a single logically necessary output $\\pi^*$. Imagining a world in which $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi'$ for some $\\pi' \\neq \\pi^*$ is therefore entertaining a *counterpossible* conditional—an antecedent that runs counter to logic, akin to asking what would happen if $6288 + 1048 \\neq 7336$.\n\nAs Yudkowsky and Soares note in Section 5, because we presently lack a general formal semantics for a logical conditioning operator $\\mathtt{true}(\\phi)$ on counterpossible propositions, P-FDT encodes subjunctive dependencies directly into the structure of the graph $G$. When the agent constructs the hypothetical $M^{\\pi \\hookrightarrow}$ via $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi)$, the intervention:\n* **Severs** any purely statistical or upstream correlations with $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ (so the agent does not think choosing a policy alters past causes like a genetic lesion or an existing termite infestation).\n* **Propagates** the chosen policy $\\pi$ forward along all directed paths in $G$ to every physical mechanism that computes or predicts $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Single-Agent Applications of P-FDT\n\nTo see how P-FDT operates in practice—and how it subtly refines action-based FDT even in single-agent sequential dilemmas—let us apply Equations (P-FDT.1) and (P-FDT.2) to the benchmark problems from the source text.\n\n### 3.1 Trivial Observation Spaces: Newcomb’s Problem\n\nIn standard dilemmas where the agent receives no informative observation during the problem, the observation space is a singleton $\\mathcal{X} = \\{\\emptyset\\}$. Here, a policy $\\pi: \\{\\emptyset\\} \\to \\mathcal{A}$ is isomorphic to a single action $a = \\pi(\\emptyset) \\in \\mathcal{A}$, so P-FDT and action-based FDT coincide identically.\n\nIn Newcomb’s problem ($\\mathcal{A} = \\{\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{twobox}\\}$), the predictor’s prediction is $99\\%$ accurate ($\\textsc{Accurate} = \\textit{accurate}$ with probability $0.99$). In P-FDT’s graph $G$, the node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ has arrows into both $\\textsc{Act}$ and $\\textsc{Prediction}$. Identifying each policy $\\pi_a$ with its constant output $a \\in \\mathcal{A}$, P-FDT evaluates:\n$$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi_{\\textit{onebox}})\\right] = 0.99(\\$1,000,000) + 0.01(\\$0) = \\$990,000,$$\n$$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi_{\\textit{twobox}})\\right] = 0.01(\\$1,001,000) + 0.99(\\$1,000) = \\$11,000.$$\nThus $\\pi^* = \\pi_{\\textit{onebox}}$, and $\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, \\emptyset) = \\textit{onebox}$.\n\n### 3.2 Nontrivial Observations: The Transparent Newcomb Problem\n\nNow consider Drescher’s **transparent Newcomb problem** (Dilemma 5 in Yudkowsky and Soares, which is structurally isomorphic to **Parfit’s hitchhiker problem** and the **mechanical blackmail problem**). Both boxes are transparent, so the agent observes the contents of Box B before choosing whether to one-box or two-box:\n$$\\mathcal{X} = \\{\\textit{full}, \\textit{empty}\\}, \\qquad \\mathcal{A} = \\{\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{twobox}\\}.$$\nThe predictor places $\\$1,000,000$ in Box B if and only if she predicts that the agent will leave Box A ($\\$$1,000) behind *upon observing that Box B is full*. The predictor is accurate with probability $0.99$.\n\nLet us compare how action-based FDT (Section 6 of the paper) and **P-FDT** analyze this problem when the agent sees $\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{full}$.\n\nIn **action-based FDT**, the graph $G^F$ (Figure 4b of the source paper) splits the agent’s decision algorithm into two separate nodes: $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P^F}, \\underline{G^F}, \\textit{full})$ and $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P^F}, \\underline{G^F}, \\textit{empty})$. When Fiona observes $\\textit{full}$, she intervenes *only* on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P^F}, \\underline{G^F}, \\textit{full})$. Because she does not intervene on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P^F}, \\underline{G^F}, \\textit{empty})$, she must treat her own behavior in the $\\textit{empty}$ branch as an external random variable governed by a prior probability $q \\coloneqq P^F(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P^F}, \\underline{G^F}, \\textit{empty}) = \\textit{twobox})$.\n\nIn **P-FDT**, by contrast, there is only *one* functional node in the graph: $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$. Its structural equations are:\n$$\\textsc{Prediction}(\\pi, \\textit{accurate}) = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } \\pi(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{onebox}, \\\\ 2 & \\text{if } \\pi(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{twobox}, \\end{cases}$$\n$$\\textsc{Prediction}(\\pi, \\textit{inaccurate}) = \\begin{cases} 2 & \\text{if } \\pi(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{onebox}, \\\\ 1 & \\text{if } \\pi(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{twobox}, \\end{cases}$$\n$$\\textsc{Box B}(1) = \\textit{full}, \\quad \\textsc{Box B}(2) = \\textit{empty}, \\quad \\textsc{Obs} = \\textsc{Box B}, \\quad \\textsc{Act}(\\pi, \\textsc{Obs}) = \\pi(\\textsc{Obs}).$$\n\nThere are $|\\mathcal{A}|^{|\\mathcal{X}|} = 2^2 = 4$ possible policies in $\\Pi$. Denote a policy by the ordered pair $\\pi = (\\pi(\\textit{full}), \\pi(\\textit{empty}))$. In Stage 1, P-FDT intervenes to set $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi$ for each of the four policies and computes expected utility across the entire probability distribution $P$ (without conditioning on $\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{full}$):\n\n1. **Policy $\\pi_{1,1} = (\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{onebox})$** (always one-box):\n   Since $\\pi_{1,1}(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{onebox}$, Box B is $\\textit{full}$ with probability $0.99$ (yielding $\\$1,000,000$) and $\\textit{empty}$ with probability $0.01$ (where the agent also one-boxes, yielding $\\$0$).\n   $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{1,1})\\right] = 0.99(\\$1,000,000) + 0.01(\\$0) = \\$990,000.$$\n\n2. **Policy $\\pi_{1,2} = (\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{twobox})$** (one-box if full, two-box if empty):\n   Since $\\pi_{1,2}(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{onebox}$, Box B is still $\\textit{full}$ with probability $0.99$ (yielding $\\$1,000,000$). With probability $0.01$, the predictor errs and Box B is $\\textit{empty}$, in which case $\\pi_{1,2}(\\textit{empty}) = \\textit{twobox}$ salvages the $\\$1,000$ in Box A.\n   $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{1,2})\\right] = 0.99(\\$1,000,000) + 0.01(\\$1,000) = \\$990,010.$$\n\n3. **Policy $\\pi_{2,1} = (\\textit{twobox}, \\textit{onebox})$** (two-box if full, one-box if empty):\n   Since $\\pi_{2,1}(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{twobox}$, the predictor predicts two-boxing. Box B is $\\textit{full}$ only when the predictor errs (probability $0.01$, yielding $\\$1,001,000$) and $\\textit{empty}$ with probability $0.99$ (yielding $\\$0$).\n   $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{2,1})\\right] = 0.01(\\$1,001,000) + 0.99(\\$0) = \\$10,010.$$\n\n4. **Policy $\\pi_{2,2} = (\\textit{twobox}, \\textit{twobox})$** (always two-box):\n   Since $\\pi_{2,2}(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{twobox}$, Box B is $\\textit{full}$ with probability $0.01$ (yielding $\\$1,001,000$) and $\\textit{empty}$ with probability $0.99$ (yielding $\\$1,000$).\n   $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{2,2})\\right] = 0.01(\\$1,001,000) + 0.99(\\$1,000) = \\$11,000.$$\n\nComparing the four values, $\\pi^*_{(P, G)} = \\pi_{1,2} = (\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{twobox})$ is the unique global maximizer. In Stage 2, upon observing $x = \\textit{full}$, the P-FDT agent outputs:\n$$\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, \\textit{full}) = \\pi_{1,2}(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{onebox}.$$\nAnd if the predictor happens to make the $1\\%$ error so that the agent observes $x = \\textit{empty}$, the agent outputs $\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, \\textit{empty}) = \\pi_{1,2}(\\textit{empty}) = \\textit{twobox}$.\n\nNotice two critical features of this derivation:\n* **Updateless Counterfactual Evaluation:** Like Wei Dai’s (2009) **upda­teless decision theory (UDT)**—and unlike **timeless decision theory (TDT)** (Yudkowsky 2010), which conditioned on $\\textsc{Obs} = x$ in addition to intervening on the logical node—P-FDT does not update its probability distribution $P$ on the observation $\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{full}$ during Stage 1. The observation $x$ enters only in Stage 2 when evaluating $\\pi^*(x)$. Because $\\pi^*$ is chosen from the global ex-ante perspective, the agent never wishes to pay for precommitments or avoid free information.\n* **Elimination of Cross-Branch Epistemic Nuisance Parameters:** In action-based FDT, evaluating $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{full})$ required introducing the prior parameter $q$ for what the agent would do at the un-intervened node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{empty})$. In P-FDT, because the intervention $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi)$ simultaneously fixes the agent’s behavior across *all* observation branches $x \\in \\mathcal{X}$, the parameter $q$ vanishes completely.\n\n### 3.3 Diagnostic Immunity: The XOR Blackmail\n\nWhat if an observation correlates with an environmental state that is *not* subjunctively downstream of the agent’s policy? Consider Soares and Fallenstein’s (2015) **XOR blackmail** (Dilemma 7 in the source text). A rumor says the agent’s house has termites ($\\textsc{Infestation} \\in \\{\\textit{termites}, \\textit{clean}\\}$, prior probability $p$ of $\\textit{termites}$, costing $\\$1,000,000$ in damages). An infallible predictor sends a blackmail letter ($\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{letter}$ vs. $\\emptyset$) demanding $\\$1,000$ if and only if either:\n1. $\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{clean}$ and $\\pi(\\textit{letter}) = \\textit{pay}$, or\n2. $\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{termites}$ and $\\pi(\\textit{letter}) = \\textit{refuse}$.\n\nIf no letter is sent ($\\textsc{Obs} = \\emptyset$), the agent has only the default action $\\textit{wait}$ ($\\mathcal{A}_{\\emptyset} = \\{\\textit{wait}\\}$). Thus, a policy $\\pi$ is completely specified by its response to the letter: $\\pi_{\\textit{pay}}$ vs. $\\pi_{\\textit{refuse}}$.\n\nIn P-FDT’s graph $G$, $\\textsc{Infestation}$ has no incoming arrows from $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$; whether the house has termites does not subjunctively depend on the agent’s policy. Intervening on $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ therefore leaves $P(\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{termites}) = p$ invariant:\n* Under $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\textit{pay}})$, the letter is sent iff $\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{clean}$ (probability $1-p$), in which case the agent pays $\\$1,000$. When $\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{termites}$ (probability $p$), no letter is sent and the agent loses $\\$1,000,000$. Thus:\n  $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\textit{pay}})\\right] = -p(\\$1,000,000) - (1-p)(\\$1,000).$$\n* Under $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\textit{refuse}})$, the letter is sent iff $\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{termites}$ (probability $p$), in which case the agent refuses to pay and loses only the $\\$1,000,000$ from termites. When $\\textsc{Infestation} = \\textit{clean}$ (probability $1-p$), no letter is sent and the agent loses $\\$0$. Thus:\n  $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\textit{refuse}})\\right] = -p(\\$1,000,000).$$\n\nFor every $p \\in [0, 1)$, $\\pi_{\\textit{refuse}}$ strictly dominates $\\pi_{\\textit{pay}}$ by $(1-p)(\\$1,000) > 0$. Therefore $\\pi^* = \\pi_{\\textit{refuse}}$, and upon receiving the letter, $\\mathrm{PFDT}(P, G, \\textit{letter}) = \\pi_{\\textit{refuse}}(\\textit{letter}) = \\textit{refuse}$. Unlike EDT, P-FDT refuses to pay for auspicious news.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Agent Dilemmas: Where P-FDT Diverges from Action-Based FDT\n\nBoth Footnote 7 and Footnote 16 state that the distinction between P-FDT (iterating over policies) and action-based FDT (Equation 4, iterating over actions) *\"only matters in multi-agent dilemmas.\"* Because Yudkowsky and Soares do not provide an example of such a dilemma in their paper, we must construct the mathematical mechanism of this divergence ourselves. (Here we explicitly go beyond the source text’s examples to demonstrate *why* the authors’ claim in Footnotes 7 and 16 holds.)\n\n### 4.1 Why Action-Based FDT Fails Under Asymmetric Observations\n\nRecall how action-based FDT achieves cooperation in the **Psychological Twin Prisoner’s Dilemma** (Dilemma 1 of the paper). In that basic dilemma, neither twin receives a distinguishing observation ($x_1 = x_2 = \\emptyset$). Consequently, both twins’ physical actions depend on the *exact same variable* $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\emptyset)$. When Twin 1 intervenes on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\emptyset)$, the intervention propagates to both her own action and her twin’s action.\n\nNow suppose the two psychological twins receive **different observations** $x_1 \\neq x_2$ before choosing their actions. Under action-based FDT (Equation 4):\n* Twin 1 (who observes $x_1$) intervenes on the node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_1})$.\n* Twin 2 (who observes $x_2$) intervenes on the node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_2})$.\n\nBecause $\\underline{x_1} \\neq \\underline{x_2}$, $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_1})$ and $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_2})$ are **two distinct nodes** in the dependency graph $G$! When Twin 1 performs the surgery $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_1}) = a)$, that intervention alters only what the algorithm outputs on input $x_1$; it does *not* intervene on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_2})$. Relative to one another, two action-based FDT twins who receive different observations are severed into causal-decision-theoretic strangers.\n\n### 4.2 Worked Example: The Asymmetric Twin Prisoner’s Dilemma\n\nLet us make this precise with a concrete dilemma:\n\n> **Dilemma (The Room-Labeled Twin Prisoner’s Dilemma).** Two psychological twins, implementing the same decision algorithm with identical world-models $(P, G)$, are placed in separate rooms: one in the Red Room ($\\textit{red}$) and one in the Blue Room ($\\textit{blue}$), assigned by a fair coin flip ($50/50$). Each twin observes the color of her own room, $x \\in \\mathcal{X} = \\{\\textit{red}, \\textit{blue}\\}$, and must choose an action $a \\in \\mathcal{A} = \\{\\textit{cooperate}, \\textit{defect}\\}$. Cooperating costs the acting twin $\\$1,000$ and delivers $\\$1,000,000$ to the *other* twin in the opposite room. Each twin cares purely egoistically about her own personal payoff $\\textsc{V}$.\n\nLet $\\textsc{Role} \\in \\{\\textit{red}, \\textit{blue}\\}$ be the agent’s own room assignment (so $\\textsc{Obs} = \\textsc{Role}$, with $P(\\textsc{Role} = \\textit{red}) = P(\\textsc{Role} = \\textit{blue}) = 0.5$), while her twin occupies the opposite room $\\overline{\\textsc{Role}} \\in \\{\\textit{blue}, \\textit{red}\\}$. If the twin in the Red Room takes action $a_{\\textit{red}}$ and the twin in the Blue Room takes action $a_{\\textit{blue}}$, the agent’s egoistic utility $\\textsc{V}$ in each state is:\n$$\\textsc{V}(\\textit{red}, a_{\\textit{red}}, a_{\\textit{blue}}) = \\$1,000,000 \\cdot \\mathbb{I}(a_{\\textit{blue}} = \\textit{cooperate}) - \\$1,000 \\cdot \\mathbb{I}(a_{\\textit{red}} = \\textit{cooperate}),$$\n$$\\textsc{V}(\\textit{blue}, a_{\\textit{red}}, a_{\\textit{blue}}) = \\$1,000,000 \\cdot \\mathbb{I}(a_{\\textit{red}} = \\textit{cooperate}) - \\$1,000 \\cdot \\mathbb{I}(a_{\\textit{blue}} = \\textit{cooperate}).$$\n\n Wait—let us see what happens if the payoff requires **joint complementarity or threshold cooperation** across the two rooms, or if each twin knows her identity from the start! Let us test both:\n\n**Case 1 (Complementary Division of Labor / Joint Threshold):** Suppose the $\\$1,000,000$ prize is awarded to each twin if and only if *both* the Red Room twin and the Blue Room twin cooperate (or, in a division-of-labor variant, if Red presses Button 1 at a cost of $\\$1,000$ *and* Blue presses Button 2 at a cost of $\\$1,000$). Otherwise, any twin who cooperates loses her $\\$1,000$ fee and gets $\\$0$.\n* **How Action-Based FDT (Equation 4) Evaluates $\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{red}$:**\n  The twin in the Red Room intervenes *only* on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red}) = a_{\\textit{red}}$, holding $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{blue})$ fixed at its prior probability $q \\coloneqq P(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{blue}) = \\textit{cooperate})$. Under $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red}) = \\textit{cooperate})$, her expected payoff is:\n  $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red}) = \\textit{cooperate})\\right] = \\$1,000,000 \\cdot q - 0.5(\\$1,000) - 0.5(\\$1,000 \\cdot q).$$\n  Under $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red}) = \\textit{defect})$, the joint threshold can never be met, so her expected payoff is $-0.5(\\$1,000 \\cdot q)$.\n  Comparing the two, $\\textit{cooperate}$ beats $\\textit{defect}$ if and only if $\\$1,000,000 \\cdot q > \\$500$, i.e., $q > 0.0005$. If both twins initially have low prior confidence that the other branch cooperates ($q < 0.0005$), or if they must coordinate on one of $K$ unlabelled actions $\\{c_1, \\dots, c_K\\}$ where no single action has prior probability above the threshold, **action-based FDT defects**! Because each node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x})$ is intervened upon in isolation, action-based FDT is trapped in the bad prior equilibrium.\n\n**Case 2 (Non-Indexical Asymmetric Twins):** Even more starkly, suppose Alice and Bob are two psychological twins who already know prior to the game who they are: Alice’s model $P_{\\text{Alice}}$ has $P_{\\text{Alice}}(\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{red}) = 1$ and measures only Alice’s payoff $\\textsc{V}_{\\text{Alice}}$, while Bob runs the same decision algorithm in the Blue Room ($\\textsc{Obs}_{\\text{Bob}} = \\textit{blue}$). In a standard Prisoner’s Dilemma where $\\textit{red}$’s cooperation costs Alice $\\$1,000$ and gives $\\$1,000,000$ to Bob, while $\\textit{blue}$’s cooperation costs Bob $\\$1,000$ and gives $\\$1,000,000$ to Alice:\n* Under **action-based FDT**, Alice intervenes on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red})$. Because Bob observes $\\textit{blue}$, Bob’s action depends on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{blue})$, not $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red})$. Thus Alice’s intervention has *zero* effect on whether Bob cooperates:\n  $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V}_{\\text{Alice}} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red}) = \\textit{cooperate})\\right] = \\$1,000,000 \\cdot q - \\$1,000,$$\n  $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\textsc{V}_{\\text{Alice}} \\;\\middle|\\; \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{red}) = \\textit{defect})\\right] = \\$1,000,000 \\cdot q.$$\n  Regardless of $q$, $\\textit{defect}$ strictly dominates $\\textit{cooperate}$ by $\\$1,000$ under action-based FDT! Both twins defect and get $\\$0$.\n\n* **How P-FDT Evaluates Both Cases:**\n  In **P-FDT**, both twins share the single policy node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi \\in \\mathcal{A}^{\\{\\textit{red}, \\textit{blue}\\}}$. The Red Room twin’s action is $\\pi(\\textit{red})$ and the Blue Room twin’s action is $\\pi(\\textit{blue})$.\n  Evaluating the four policies $\\pi = (\\pi(\\textit{red}), \\pi(\\textit{blue}))$ under the symmetric world-model $(P, G)$:\n  1. $\\pi_{C,C} = (\\textit{cooperate}, \\textit{cooperate})$: Both twins cooperate, yielding $\\$1,000,000 - \\$1,000 = \\$999,000$.\n  2. $\\pi_{C,D} = (\\textit{cooperate}, \\textit{defect})$: Expected utility is $0.5(-\\$1,000) + 0.5(\\$1,000,000) = \\$499,500$ in the additive Prisoner’s Dilemma (and $-\\$500$ in the joint-threshold game).\n  3. $\\pi_{D,C} = (\\textit{defect}, \\textit{cooperate})$: Expected utility is $0.5(\\$1,000,000) + 0.5(-\\$1,000) = \\$499,500$ (and $-\\$500$ in the joint-threshold game).\n  4. $\\pi_{D,D} = (\\textit{defect}, \\textit{defect})$: Both twins defect, yielding $\\$0$.\n\n  Regardless of any prior beliefs $q$, P-FDT’s intervention $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi)$ simultaneously controls $\\pi(\\textit{red})$ and $\\pi(\\textit{blue})$. The joint policy $\\pi^* = \\pi_{C,C}$ uniquely maximizes expected utility at $\\$999,000$. In Stage 2, the Red Room twin computes $\\pi_{C,C}(\\textit{red}) = \\textit{cooperate}$ and the Blue Room twin computes $\\pi_{C,C}(\\textit{blue}) = \\textit{cooperate}$.\n\n| Decision Theory | Node(s) Intervened Upon | Conditioning on $\\textsc{Obs} = x$? | Transparent Newcomb ($\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{full}$) | Room-Labeled Twin PD ($x_1 = \\textit{red}, x_2 = \\textit{blue}$) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **EDT** | None (Bayesian conditioning on $\\textsc{Act} = a$) | Yes | $\\textit{twobox}$ $(\\$1,000)$ | $\\textit{defect}$ (if independent conditional on room) |\n| **CDT** | Physical node $\\textsc{Act} = a$ | Yes | $\\textit{twobox}$ $(\\$1,000)$ | $\\textit{defect}$ $(\\$0)$ |\n| **Action-Based FDT** | Observation-specific node $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x}) = a$ | No | $\\textit{onebox}$ $(\\$1,000,000)$ | Fails when $x_1 \\neq x_2$ severs nodes |\n| **P-FDT** | Global policy node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi$, then apply $\\pi(x)$ | No | $\\textit{onebox}$ $(\\$1,000,000)$ | $\\textit{cooperate}$ $(\\$999,000)$ |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Open Questions in the Source Text and Operational Interpretations\n\nBecause Yudkowsky and Soares compress their description of **P-FDT** into two brief footnotes, several foundational questions are left unresolved by the source text. To make P-FDT fully operational for unfamiliar cases, we must identify these ambiguities and formulate principled mathematical resolutions, clearly distinguishing the source paper’s claims from our own completions.\n\n### 5.1 What Is the Exact Domain of a Policy? ($\\mathcal{X}$ vs. $\\mathcal{M} \\times \\mathcal{X}$)\n\n**The Uncertainty in the Source Text:**\nThere is a subtle tension between Footnote 7 and Footnote 16:\n* Footnote 7 defines policies as *\"mappings from observations to actions\"* ($\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$).\n* Footnote 16 says that the algorithm *\"intervenes on the node $\\mathrm{FDT}(-)$ to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\"*\n\nIn Equation 4 of the paper, the inputs to the $\\mathrm{FDT}$ procedure are $(P, G, x)$—both the world-model $M = (P, G)$ and the observation $x$. Does a P-FDT policy take *only* the observation $x$ as its input (so the node is $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ and $\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$), or does it take the entire tuple $(P, G, x)$ as its input (so the node is $\\textsc{pfdt}()$ and $\\Pi: \\mathcal{M} \\times \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$)?\n\nObserve why this matters in the source paper’s own **smoking lesion problem** (Section 6, Figure 3). In Yudkowsky and Soares’s formalization of the smoking lesion problem, the agent receives *no* observation ($x = \\emptyset$). Instead, the lesion alters the agent’s utility function ($\\mathcal{U}_S$ if she has the lesion, $\\mathcal{U}_R$ if she does not), which changes her probability distribution over $\\textsc{V}$ from $P_S^F$ to $P_R^F$. Thus, in Figure 3 of the paper, the two branches are represented by $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P_S^F}, \\underline{G^F})$ and $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P_R^F}, \\underline{G^F})$.\n\n**Operational Interpretation and Proposal:**\nIf we want P-FDT to unify cross-observation dependencies and cross-model dependencies without forcing an agent with preference $\\mathcal{U}_S$ to optimize for a counterfactual utility function $\\mathcal{U}_R$ that she does not endorse, we should adopt the following operational rule:\n1. **Separate Epistemic Inputs from Evaluative Preferences:** Factor the world-model into an epistemic belief state $M = (P, G)$ over world-states $\\mathcal{O}$ and a utility function $\\mathcal{U}$. Whenever a sensory cue, inclination (such as a craving to smoke in the lesion problem, Eells 1984), or indexical role varies across branches of a dilemma, encode that variation inside the observation/input space $\\mathcal{X}$ relative to a common prior $(P, G)$.\n2. **Keep $(P, G)$ as Fixed Indexical Parameters of the Node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$:** Define the policy space as $\\Pi = \\mathcal{A}^{\\mathcal{X}}$. This matches Footnote 7’s definition ($\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$) while satisfying Footnote 16: the ex-ante structure of the dilemma is captured by $(P, G)$, and all runtime contingencies are passed as arguments $x \\in \\mathcal{X}$ to the policy $\\pi(x)$.\n\n### 5.2 Indexical Priors in Multi-Agent Dilemmas\n\n**The Uncertainty in the Source Text:**\nIn Section 4.2, we noted that if two agents in an asymmetric game have distinct observations $x_1 = \\textit{red}$ and $x_2 = \\textit{blue}$, P-FDT achieves mutual cooperation when evaluated under a prior $P$ that places positive probability on both indexical roles ($\\textsc{Role} \\in \\{\\textit{red}, \\textit{blue}\\}$). What if an agent is built *after* already being placed in the Red Room, so that from the very beginning of her existence she knows with certainty that she is the Red Player? If $P(\\textsc{Role} = \\textit{red}) = 1$ in her prior $P$, then even if she chooses a full policy $\\pi: \\{\\textit{red}, \\textit{blue}\\} \\to \\mathcal{A}$, changing $\\pi(\\textit{blue})$ only incurs a cost for the Blue Player and yields a benefit to the Red Player! If the Red Player maximizes *only* the Red Player’s utility, she would choose the asymmetric policy $\\pi(\\textit{red}) = \\textit{defect}, \\pi(\\textit{blue}) = \\textit{cooperate}$—while the Blue Player, maximizing only the Blue Player’s utility, would choose $\\pi(\\textit{red}) = \\textit{cooperate}, \\pi(\\textit{blue}) = \\textit{defect}$, contradicting the assumption that both players implement the same function $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$!\n\n**Operational Interpretation and Proposal:**\nThis reveals a fundamental mathematical constraint required to apply P-FDT to multi-agent problems: **two agents share the same P-FDT node $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ if and only if they evaluate policies from the same prior $(P, G)$**. To apply P-FDT in asymmetric multi-agent games where players occupy distinct roles $x_1, \\dots, x_n$:\n* The prior $P$ used in $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ must be a *pre-indexical* (or \"veil-of-ignorance\") prior in which the agent’s role $\\textsc{Obs} \\in \\{x_1, \\dots, x_n\\}$ is an unconditioned random variable (e.g., uniform over the $n$ copies), and the agent’s personal utility $\\textsc{V} = \\mathcal{U}(\\textsc{Outcome}, \\textsc{Obs})$ is evaluated in expectation across those roles.\n* Once each copy $i$ observes her actual indexical role $x_i$ in Stage 2, she executes $\\pi^*_{(P, G)}(x_i)$. Because Stage 1 is identical for all copies, every copy computes the exact same optimal policy $\\pi^*_{(P, G)}$, preserving subjunctive dependence across the entire coalition.\n\n### 5.3 Tie-Breaking on Zero-Probability Observations and Computational Complexity\n\n**The Uncertainty in the Source Text:**\nWhat if a policy’s action at one observation $x_1$ causes another observation $x_2$ to have probability *zero* under $P(\\cdot \\mid \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi))$?\nFor instance, return to the Transparent Newcomb problem (Section 3.2), and suppose the predictor is **infallible** ($P(\\textsc{Accurate} = \\textit{accurate}) = 1$). Then:\n* For any policy with $\\pi(\\textit{full}) = \\textit{onebox}$, Box B is *certain* to be full ($P(\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{empty} \\mid \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi)) = 0$).\n* Consequently, $\\pi_{1,1} = (\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{onebox})$ and $\\pi_{1,2} = (\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{twobox})$ yield the exact same expected utility of $\\$1,000,000$!\nMore importantly, what if a policy $\\pi$ sets $P(\\textsc{Obs} = x \\mid \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi)) = 0$, and yet the agent *actually observes* $x$ (say, in an infallible Mechanical Blackmail problem where the blackmailer only sends a letter if she predicts $\\pi(\\textit{letter}) = \\textit{pay}$, so under $\\pi_{\\textit{refuse}}$ the event $\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{letter}$ has probability zero)?\n\n**Operational Interpretation and Proposal:**\n1. **Well-Definedness on Measure-Zero Observations:** Unlike EDT (Equation 2) and CDT (Equation 3), which divide by $P(\\textsc{Obs} = x)$ via Bayesian conditioning and are therefore mathematically undefined when $P(\\textsc{Obs} = x) = 0$, **P-FDT remains completely well-defined on probability-zero observations**! Even if $P(\\textsc{Obs} = \\textit{letter} \\mid \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\textit{refuse}})) = 0$, the unconditional expectation $\\mathbb{E}[\\textsc{V} \\mid \\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\textit{refuse}})]$ is well-defined, and Stage 2 simply evaluates the function $\\pi_{\\textit{refuse}}(\\textit{letter}) = \\textit{refuse}$.\n2. **Trembling-Hand / $\\varepsilon$-Perturbation for Unreached Branches:** When an infallible predictor makes two policies $\\pi_{1,1}$ and $\\pi_{1,2}$ tie on expected utility because the $\\textit{empty}$ branch has probability zero under both, we can break ties cleanly by taking the limit as predictor error $\\varepsilon \\to 0^+$ (analogous to Selten’s trembling-hand perfection). As shown in Section 3.2, for every $\\varepsilon > 0$, $\\pi_{1,2} = (\\textit{onebox}, \\textit{twobox})$ beats $\\pi_{1,1}$ by $\\varepsilon \\cdot \\$1,000 > 0$, uniquely selecting $\\pi_{1,2}$ in the $\\varepsilon \\to 0^+$ limit.\n3. **Tractable Policy Evaluation:** Finally, whereas action-based FDT maximizes over $|\\mathcal{A}|$ actions, Stage 1 of P-FDT maximizes over $|\\mathcal{A}|^{|\\mathcal{X}|}$ policies. In practice, an agent need not enumerate all $|\\mathcal{A}|^{|\\mathcal{X}|}$ mappings: she only needs to jointly optimize over the Equivalence Classes of observations $\\mathcal{X}_{\\text{rel}} \\subseteq \\mathcal{X}$ that are subjunctively coupled through predictors or other agents, while solving independent observation branches pointwise.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\n**Policy functional decision theory (P-FDT)** brings the philosophical vision of functional decision theory to its natural mathematical completion. Where CDT intervenes on physical actions and updates on observations—thereby severing logical links to predictors, twins, and past commitments—and where EDT conditions on actions—thereby succumbing to spurious statistical correlations and blackmail—P-FDT elevates the unit of counterfactual intervention to the **policy** $\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$.\n\nBy first selecting the optimal policy $\\pi^*_{(P, G)}$ via a global subjunctive intervention $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi)$ on the unconditioned world-model, and then applying $\\pi^*_{(P, G)}(x)$ to the realized observation $x$, P-FDT achieves three things simultaneously:\n1. It preserves every triumph of standard FDT in single-agent dilemmas—one-boxing in Newcomb’s problem and the transparent Newcomb problem, smoking in the smoking lesion problem, and resisting both XOR and mechanical blackmail.\n2. It eliminates arbitrary prior assumptions about how the agent would act in counterfactual observation branches.\n3. It enables seamless coordination across multiple agents or copies who receive distinct observations, fulfilling the promise noted in Footnotes 7 and 16 of Yudkowsky and Soares (2017).\n\nWhile deep open problems remain—most notably developing a general formal theory of counterpossible inference to replace ad-hoc graphical specification of $G$—P-FDT provides a mathematically rigorous, unified decision procedure for rational agency in a world populated by predictors and peers.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n* Ahmed, Arif. 2014. *Evidence, Decision and Causality*. Cambridge University Press.\n* Dai, Wei. 2009. \"Towards a New Decision Theory.\" *LessWrong*.\n* Drescher, Gary L. 2006. *Good and Real: Demystifying Paradoxes from Physics to Ethics*. MIT Press.\n* Eells, Ellery. 1984. \"Metatickles and the Dynamics of Deliberation.\" *Theory and Decision* 17 (1): 71–95.\n* Gibbard, Allan, and William L. Harper. 1978. \"Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility.\" In *Foundations and Applications of Decision Theory*, vol. 1, edited by C. A. Hooker, J. J. Leach, and E. F. McClennen. D. Reidel.\n* Joyce, James M. 1999. *The Foundations of Causal Decision Theory*. Cambridge University Press.\n* Kleene, Stephen Cole. 1938. \"On Notation for Ordinal Numbers.\" *Journal of Symbolic Logic* 3 (4): 150–155.\n* Lewis, David. 1979. \"Prisoners' Dilemma is a Newcomb Problem.\" *Philosophy & Public Affairs* 8 (3): 235–240.\n* Lewis, David. 1981. \"Causal Decision Theory.\" *Australasian Journal of Philosophy* 59 (1): 5–30.\n* Nozick, Robert. 1969. \"Newcomb's Problem and Two Principles of Choice.\" In *Essays in Honor of Carl G. Hempel*, edited by Nicholas Rescher, 114–146. D. Reidel.\n* Parfit, Derek. 1986. *Reasons and Persons*. Oxford University Press.\n* Pearl, Judea. 1996. \"Causation, Action, and Counterfactuals.\" In *6th Conference on Theoretical Aspects of Rationality and Knowledge (TARK-'96)*, 51–73.\n* Pearl, Judea. 2000. *Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference*. Cambridge University Press.\n* Skyrms, Brian. 1980. *Causal Necessity: A Pragmatic Investigation of the Necessity of Laws*. Yale University Press.\n* Soares, Nate, and Benja Fallenstein. 2015. \"Toward Idealized Decision Theory.\" *arXiv:1507.01986*.\n* Yudkowsky, Eliezer. 2010. *Timeless Decision Theory*. The Singularity Institute.\n* Yudkowsky, Eliezer, and Nate Soares. 2017. \"Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality.\" *arXiv:1710.05060v2*.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Defining the Approach**\n\nI'm now focusing on defining the approach for a mathematical essay on P-FDT. The objective is to produce a 2400-3000 word blog post from a researcher's perspective, explaining P-FDT and its application. The primary source material, a research paper, is my key reference.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Core Principles**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on footnote 16 and related passages in Yudkowsky & Soares' work, particularly as they relate to policy selection. This is becoming the cornerstone of my analysis. I will be incorporating the instructions and constraints, and framing them from the perspective of a researcher penning a technical blog post, as specified. The mathematical rigor and clarity of the argument for policy selection is key.\n\n\n**Reviewing Key Concepts**\n\nI am now focusing on differentiating claims made in the source material from any supplementary assumptions I might propose. My goal is to produce a readable mathematical essay of approximately 2400-3000 words that explains P-FDT in depth, making it complete and cohesive while avoiding exhaustive coverage. I plan to use \"P-FDT\" consistently and identify any other decision theories by their distinct names. I'm prioritizing the policy-selection passage in footnote 16 as the primary reference, and maintaining the fidelity of quotations. I will be incorporating the instructions and constraints, and framing them from the perspective of a researcher penning a technical blog post, as specified. The mathematical rigor and clarity of the argument for policy selection is key.\n\n\n**Clarifying Intervention Points**\n\nMy focus is shifting to precisely defining the intervention point in FDT, specifically how a policy choice differs from an action selection. Footnote 16 highlights this difference, and it's key to understanding the nuances of multi-agent dilemmas. My goal is to clearly explain this distinction within the framework of a technical blog post, maintaining mathematical rigor and clarity, using the referenced equations and the policy-selection passage as my guide. I'm carefully considering how this affects my earlier framing.\n\n\n**Contrasting FDT Variants**\n\nI'm now contrasting standard FDT with P-FDT, focusing on how P-FDT intervenes at the policy level rather than the action level. This involves analyzing the difference between action-based FDT, described by Equation 4, and how P-FDT operates on observation-specific nodes, like in the Mechanical Blackmail scenario. I will consider the implications of this distinction, especially in scenarios with multiple nodes. I will also incorporate mentions of Wei Dai's UDT and Yudkowsky's TDT from the paper.\n\n\n**Analyzing Policy Selection**\n\nI'm now focusing on how P-FDT explicitly intervenes on a policy, distinguishing this approach from action-based intervention. Specifically, I'm examining how $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ sets a policy $\\pi$ via $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi)$. I see that once the optimal policy $\\pi^*$ is selected, the agent applies her inputs $x$ to get action $a = \\pi^*(x)$. This is crucial for clarifying the nuances of policy selection within the essay. I'm investigating the potential ambiguity between Footnotes 7 and 16.\n\n\n**Clarifying Agent Inputs**\n\nI'm now thinking about the \"inputs\" to the FDT procedure versus the policy function. Footnote 7 defines a policy as a mapping from observations to actions, or $\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$. Footnote 16 then says FDT intervenes to select a policy. In Equation 4, inputs to the decision procedure are $(P, G, x)$. I'm considering scenarios with multiple agents with different world models or utilities, to clarify input mapping.\n\n\n**Investigating Agent Knowledge**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications when an agent's world model or preferences are uncertain. I'm exploring the domain of the policy function, specifically whether it maps directly from observations or needs an additional component representing the agent's internal model, which is an open question. I'm analyzing the differences and implications of the domain being $\\mathcal{X}$ or $(\\mathcal{M} \\times \\mathcal{X})$.\n\n\n**Examining Model Dependencies**\n\nI'm now wrestling with the formalization of the observation-policy and the generalized input-policy, particularly in multi-agent scenarios or those with interdependent branches. I'm exploring the implications of varying world models or inputs. I'm also planning to investigate how action-based FDT handles cases like the Smoking Lesion problem.\n\n\n**Constructing New Scenarios**\n\nI'm now building concrete examples to explore scenarios with joint behavior across multiple observations and agents. I'm focusing on a multi-agent dilemma where different agents receive different observations, and their joint actions determine the payoffs. This includes situations with asymmetric observations, and I'm developing a test case that incorporates these conditions.\n\n\n**Developing a Dilemma**\n\nI'm now formulating a specific multi-agent dilemma where two agents, each observing a different $x$, must coordinate actions with asymmetric rewards. I'm focusing on a scenario where both agents working yields a substantial payoff, but each has an individual cost to cooperate. The goal is to mathematically formalize this test case to highlight the nuances of P-FDT in scenarios of interdependent action.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Coordination Dilemmas**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a critical point: how action-based FDT handles coordination games with multiple observations. I see that when an agent observes $x_1$, she only intervenes on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_1})$, holding $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_2})$ fixed. I need to clarify the implications of this in a pure coordination game.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Epistemic Uncertainty**\n\nI'm now focusing on how P-FDT addresses epistemic uncertainty across observation branches, especially in coordination scenarios. I'm unpacking the implications of action-based FDT's handling of separate observations, which is different from how P-FDT can intervene on a single policy node. I need to clarify why epistemic uncertainty ($q$) matters in some FDT contexts, but not others.\n\n\n\n**Elaborating on Dual Observations**\n\nI'm now focusing on the implications of P-FDT simultaneously specifying actions across multiple observations. This is in contrast to action-based FDT, which intervenes separately on distinct nodes. I see that P-FDT jointly optimizes policies in multi-agent scenarios like the twin agent example, making epistemic uncertainty ($q$) irrelevant. This clarifies why Footnotes 7 and 16 highlighted this distinction.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Dilemma Failures**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on why action-based FDT fails in multi-agent dilemmas when agents have differing observations, unlike P-FDT. I see that action-based FDT treats another agent's node as causally independent of its own intervention. In a Prisoner's Dilemma, this leads to suboptimal outcomes for twins in separate rooms, unlike P-FDT.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Dilemma Failures**\n\nI'm zeroing in on why action-based FDT fails in multi-agent dilemmas when agents have differing observations, unlike P-FDT. I see that action-based FDT treats another agent's node as causally independent of its own intervention. In a Prisoner's Dilemma, this leads to suboptimal outcomes for twins in separate rooms, unlike P-FDT. I will incorporate this finding.\n\n\n\n**Refining Utility Maximization**\n\nI'm now clarifying the nature of utility maximization in the twin scenario. With P-FDT, if Twin 1 knows she is in room 1, and maximizes her own utility $\\textsc{V}_1$, should she maximize expected utility before knowing the room? Or does she have an indexical utility function $\\textsc{V}_1$? I'm exploring the implications for the policy selection.\n\n\n**Tracing Policy Choices**\n\nI'm now tracing the implications of both policy selection possibilities: an agent as a global entity with two copies versus one with indexical utility. I see how Equation 4 works without conditioning on an observation. In Transparent Newcomb, Fiona maximizes utility given a full box observation, exploring how this framework handles indexical information and agent identity. I'm focusing on the effect of the prior on policy selection.\n\n\n**Refining Twin Scenarios**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications of the prior in Twin scenarios, particularly concerning action-based FDT. I've noted a typo in the inline display equation in the paper, which has implications for how action-based FDT is treated. The prior distribution $P$ is never conditioned on the observation. I see this has implications for Transparent Newcomb.\n\n\n**Analyzing Twin Asymmetries**\n\nI'm now investigating an asymmetric Prisoner's Dilemma variant with twins, focusing on the differences between action-based FDT and P-FDT when each twin observes a different room. The goal is to mathematically formalize this test case, exploring scenarios with and without indexical uncertainty, to understand how P-FDT's policy-based intervention handles asymmetric observations. I'm starting by analyzing the scenario where one twin pays $1000 to reward the other with $1,000,000.\n\n\n**Clarifying Asymmetric Twins**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications for action-based FDT in the asymmetric twin Prisoner's Dilemma. I see an agent's expected utility depends on her observation, for example, Room A. In the scenario, if an agent cares only about their own payoff, then utility is still indexical to the branch in which her observation is true. This means that an agent's payoff depends on what the other agent does, given the observation.\n\n\n**Revising Utility Functions**\n\nI'm now revising the utility function to clarify how action-based FDT handles symmetric utility. In the asymmetric twin Prisoner's Dilemma, an agent's expected utility depends on her observation, such as Room A. If an agent cares only about her own payoff, her utility is still indexical to the branch in which her observation is true. This means the payoff depends on the other agent's action, given the observation. I am further clarifying a framework for dual agents.\n\n\n**Clarifying Twin Payoffs**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications of the utility function in the context of symmetric agents and asymmetric payoffs. I've realized agents can share the same decision function because their identity is part of the input, allowing each to maximize their individual utility. Considering Alice's and Bob's perspectives, I'm analyzing the implications of this approach.\n\n\n**Modeling Twin Observations**\n\nI'm now modeling a Stag Hunt with twins who have different roles, receiving distinct observations. Each twin, observing their respective role, can choose between Stag and Hare. If both choose Stag, they receive a substantial reward, but there's a smaller reward for Hare and zero for Stag if the other chooses Hare. I am formalizing this test case now.\n\n\n\n**Formalizing Epistemic Impact**\n\nI'm formalizing the impact of Twin 1's action when using action-based FDT, and I now realize the impact of the prior $P$ on Twin 2's action is minimal. If Twin 1 intervenes and chooses \"Stag\", Twin 2 is unlikely to change her mind, and Twin 1 doesn't influence $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{Role 2})$. The prior's weight ($q$) and the action-based intervention make cooperation unlikely, leading to suboptimal outcomes.\n\n\n**Modeling Coordination Failure**\n\nI'm now formalizing a division of labor scenario where each twin must select a frequency, with coordination required for payoff. Action-based FDT fails because one twin's action doesn't affect the other's, as their interventions are independent. The prior's weight ($q$) and the action-based intervention make cooperation unlikely, leading to suboptimal outcomes.\n\n\n**Refining the Twin Scenarios**\n\nI'm revisiting the asymmetric twin coordination problem, specifically when an agent is certain of their role and how the policy is implemented, contrasting it with action-based FDT. I'm focusing on the epistemic impact when one twin can only impact their action, which results in the failure of action-based FDT to produce cooperation.\n\n\n**Formalizing Subjunctive Dependencies**\n\nNow I'm formalizing the framework, focusing on the distinction between action-based and policy-based intervention, emphasizing subjunctive dependencies. I'm building on Footnotes 7 and 16, and contrasting EDT, CDT, standard FDT, and P-FDT using the primitives from the paper, like outcomes, actions, observations, and utilities. I'm focusing on the policy space to differentiate the two intervention types.\n\n\n**Formalizing the Framework**\n\nNow, I'm defining the framework using outcomes, actions, observations, and utilities from the paper, building on Footnotes 7 and 16. I'm focusing on the policy space to differentiate action-based and policy-based interventions, especially the graphical mechanics of P-FDT in contrast with other decision theories. I'm building on the distinction between action-based and policy-based intervention.\n\n\n**Elaborating on Policy Selection**\n\nI'm expanding my analysis to incorporate Kleene's second recursion theorem and its relation to counterpossible interventions, treating logical variables as stand-ins for $\\mathtt{true}(\\phi)$. I'm also planning to work through examples like Transparent Newcomb, Mechanical Blackmail, and Parfit's Hitchhiker, and show how P-FDT handles policy selection within these. The aim is to illustrate P-FDT's evaluation of single-agent dilemmas, highlighting updatelessness and subjunctive dependence.\n\n\n**Calculating Expected Utility**\n\nI'm now calculating exact expected utilities for Transparent Newcomb using P-FDT to demonstrate its superiority over action-based FDT. Specifically, I'm showing that $\\pi_{1,2}$ (one-box if full, two-box if empty) uniquely maximizes expected utility at \\$990,010, beating alternatives. This highlights the subtle mathematical advantage of policy selection even in single-agent scenarios.\n\n\n**Contrasting Newcomb Scenarios**\n\nNow I'm focusing on contrasting Transparent Newcomb under action-based and P-FDT frameworks. I can see that in action-based FDT, the prior parameter, $q$, affects the evaluation of the \"full\" box decision, as the two decision branches are independent. In P-FDT, this epistemic parameter is completely eliminated, as the policy sets the actions across all observations. I'm now working to formalize how this difference affects utility maximization in single-agent scenarios. I will also incorporate clear examples in Section 4 to distinguish the paper's claim.\n\n\n**Formalizing Decision Failures**\n\nNow I'm formalizing the Asymmetric Twin Dilemma, where action-based FDT fails because interventions are on independent branches.  P-FDT, however, simultaneously adjusts actions across both agents, leading to cooperation. This highlights why Equation 4 leads to suboptimal outcomes due to disjoint observations in multi-agent scenarios. The framework for policy selection is now clearer.\n\n\n**Distinguishing Policy Domains**\n\nI'm now clarifying the domain of the policy function. I'm focusing on whether a policy is a mapping from observations to actions ($\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$) or requires an additional component to the input ($\\Pi: \\mathcal{M} \\times \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$). I'm using the Smoking Lesion problem and multi-agent dilemmas to contrast observation policies with meta-policies. I'm also considering self-locating beliefs, and utility aggregation.\n\n\n**Clarifying Payoff Aggregation**\n\nI'm now focusing on the implications of payoff aggregation in multi-agent P-FDT scenarios, especially when agents have distinct observations and indexical identities. I'm exploring how the prior $P$ factors into utility evaluation before observations are made. I'm clarifying how this approach, where indexical identity is encoded in the observation, enables coordination without requiring altruistic utility functions. I'm also planning to clarify how to resolve dependencies.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Global Perspectives**\n\nI'm now focusing on a succinct conclusion for the essay. I see that P-FDT provides the \"global perspective\" that FDT requires. The essay's conclusion will emphasize P-FDT's fulfillment of FDT's core goal. I'll need to briefly summarize the advantages in a way that remains technically sound.\n\n\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Defining the Approach**\n\nI'm now focusing on defining the approach for a mathematical essay on P-FDT. The objective is to produce a 2400-3000 word blog post from a researcher's perspective, explaining P-FDT and its application. The primary source material, a research paper, is my key reference.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Core Principles**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on footnote 16 and related passages in Yudkowsky & Soares' work, particularly as they relate to policy selection. This is becoming the cornerstone of my analysis. I will be incorporating the instructions and constraints, and framing them from the perspective of a researcher penning a technical blog post, as specified. The mathematical rigor and clarity of the argument for policy selection is key.\n\n\n**Reviewing Key Concepts**\n\nI am now focusing on differentiating claims made in the source material from any supplementary assumptions I might propose. My goal is to produce a readable mathematical essay of approximately 2400-3000 words that explains P-FDT in depth, making it complete and cohesive while avoiding exhaustive coverage. I plan to use \"P-FDT\" consistently and identify any other decision theories by their distinct names. I'm prioritizing the policy-selection passage in footnote 16 as the primary reference, and maintaining the fidelity of quotations. I will be incorporating the instructions and constraints, and framing them from the perspective of a researcher penning a technical blog post, as specified. The mathematical rigor and clarity of the argument for policy selection is key.\n\n\n**Clarifying Intervention Points**\n\nMy focus is shifting to precisely defining the intervention point in FDT, specifically how a policy choice differs from an action selection. Footnote 16 highlights this difference, and it's key to understanding the nuances of multi-agent dilemmas. My goal is to clearly explain this distinction within the framework of a technical blog post, maintaining mathematical rigor and clarity, using the referenced equations and the policy-selection passage as my guide. I'm carefully considering how this affects my earlier framing.\n\n\n**Contrasting FDT Variants**\n\nI'm now contrasting standard FDT with P-FDT, focusing on how P-FDT intervenes at the policy level rather than the action level. This involves analyzing the difference between action-based FDT, described by Equation 4, and how P-FDT operates on observation-specific nodes, like in the Mechanical Blackmail scenario. I will consider the implications of this distinction, especially in scenarios with multiple nodes. I will also incorporate mentions of Wei Dai's UDT and Yudkowsky's TDT from the paper.\n\n\n**Analyzing Policy Selection**\n\nI'm now focusing on how P-FDT explicitly intervenes on a policy, distinguishing this approach from action-based intervention. Specifically, I'm examining how $\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ sets a policy $\\pi$ via $\\mathtt{do}(\\textsc{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi)$. I see that once the optimal policy $\\pi^*$ is selected, the agent applies her inputs $x$ to get action $a = \\pi^*(x)$. This is crucial for clarifying the nuances of policy selection within the essay. I'm investigating the potential ambiguity between Footnotes 7 and 16.\n\n\n**Clarifying Agent Inputs**\n\nI'm now thinking about the \"inputs\" to the FDT procedure versus the policy function. Footnote 7 defines a policy as a mapping from observations to actions, or $\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$. Footnote 16 then says FDT intervenes to select a policy. In Equation 4, inputs to the decision procedure are $(P, G, x)$. I'm considering scenarios with multiple agents with different world models or utilities, to clarify input mapping.\n\n\n**Investigating Agent Knowledge**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications when an agent's world model or preferences are uncertain. I'm exploring the domain of the policy function, specifically whether it maps directly from observations or needs an additional component representing the agent's internal model, which is an open question. I'm analyzing the differences and implications of the domain being $\\mathcal{X}$ or $(\\mathcal{M} \\times \\mathcal{X})$.\n\n\n**Examining Model Dependencies**\n\nI'm now wrestling with the formalization of the observation-policy and the generalized input-policy, particularly in multi-agent scenarios or those with interdependent branches. I'm exploring the implications of varying world models or inputs. I'm also planning to investigate how action-based FDT handles cases like the Smoking Lesion problem.\n\n\n**Constructing New Scenarios**\n\nI'm now building concrete examples to explore scenarios with joint behavior across multiple observations and agents. I'm focusing on a multi-agent dilemma where different agents receive different observations, and their joint actions determine the payoffs. This includes situations with asymmetric observations, and I'm developing a test case that incorporates these conditions.\n\n\n**Developing a Dilemma**\n\nI'm now formulating a specific multi-agent dilemma where two agents, each observing a different $x$, must coordinate actions with asymmetric rewards. I'm focusing on a scenario where both agents working yields a substantial payoff, but each has an individual cost to cooperate. The goal is to mathematically formalize this test case to highlight the nuances of P-FDT in scenarios of interdependent action.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Coordination Dilemmas**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a critical point: how action-based FDT handles coordination games with multiple observations. I see that when an agent observes $x_1$, she only intervenes on $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_1})$, holding $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x_2})$ fixed. I need to clarify the implications of this in a pure coordination game.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Epistemic Uncertainty**\n\nI'm now focusing on how P-FDT addresses epistemic uncertainty across observation branches, especially in coordination scenarios. I'm unpacking the implications of action-based FDT's handling of separate observations, which is different from how P-FDT can intervene on a single policy node. I need to clarify why epistemic uncertainty ($q$) matters in some FDT contexts, but not others.\n\n\n\n**Elaborating on Dual Observations**\n\nI'm now focusing on the implications of P-FDT simultaneously specifying actions across multiple observations. This is in contrast to action-based FDT, which intervenes separately on distinct nodes. I see that P-FDT jointly optimizes policies in multi-agent scenarios like the twin agent example, making epistemic uncertainty ($q$) irrelevant. This clarifies why Footnotes 7 and 16 highlighted this distinction.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Dilemma Failures**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on why action-based FDT fails in multi-agent dilemmas when agents have differing observations, unlike P-FDT. I see that action-based FDT treats another agent's node as causally independent of its own intervention. In a Prisoner's Dilemma, this leads to suboptimal outcomes for twins in separate rooms, unlike P-FDT.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Dilemma Failures**\n\nI'm zeroing in on why action-based FDT fails in multi-agent dilemmas when agents have differing observations, unlike P-FDT. I see that action-based FDT treats another agent's node as causally independent of its own intervention. In a Prisoner's Dilemma, this leads to suboptimal outcomes for twins in separate rooms, unlike P-FDT. I will incorporate this finding.\n\n\n\n**Refining Utility Maximization**\n\nI'm now clarifying the nature of utility maximization in the twin scenario. With P-FDT, if Twin 1 knows she is in room 1, and maximizes her own utility $\\textsc{V}_1$, should she maximize expected utility before knowing the room? Or does she have an indexical utility function $\\textsc{V}_1$? I'm exploring the implications for the policy selection.\n\n\n**Tracing Policy Choices**\n\nI'm now tracing the implications of both policy selection possibilities: an agent as a global entity with two copies versus one with indexical utility. I see how Equation 4 works without conditioning on an observation. In Transparent Newcomb, Fiona maximizes utility given a full box observation, exploring how this framework handles indexical information and agent identity. I'm focusing on the effect of the prior on policy selection.\n\n\n**Refining Twin Scenarios**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications of the prior in Twin scenarios, particularly concerning action-based FDT. I've noted a typo in the inline display equation in the paper, which has implications for how action-based FDT is treated. The prior distribution $P$ is never conditioned on the observation. I see this has implications for Transparent Newcomb.\n\n\n**Analyzing Twin Asymmetries**\n\nI'm now investigating an asymmetric Prisoner's Dilemma variant with twins, focusing on the differences between action-based FDT and P-FDT when each twin observes a different room. The goal is to mathematically formalize this test case, exploring scenarios with and without indexical uncertainty, to understand how P-FDT's policy-based intervention handles asymmetric observations. I'm starting by analyzing the scenario where one twin pays $1000 to reward the other with $1,000,000.\n\n\n**Clarifying Asymmetric Twins**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications for action-based FDT in the asymmetric twin Prisoner's Dilemma. I see an agent's expected utility depends on her observation, for example, Room A. In the scenario, if an agent cares only about their own payoff, then utility is still indexical to the branch in which her observation is true. This means that an agent's payoff depends on what the other agent does, given the observation.\n\n\n**Revising Utility Functions**\n\nI'm now revising the utility function to clarify how action-based FDT handles symmetric utility. In the asymmetric twin Prisoner's Dilemma, an agent's expected utility depends on her observation, such as Room A. If an agent cares only about her own payoff, her utility is still indexical to the branch in which her observation is true. This means the payoff depends on the other agent's action, given the observation. I am further clarifying a framework for dual agents.\n\n\n**Clarifying Twin Payoffs**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications of the utility function in the context of symmetric agents and asymmetric payoffs. I've realized agents can share the same decision function because their identity is part of the input, allowing each to maximize their individual utility. Considering Alice's and Bob's perspectives, I'm analyzing the implications of this approach.\n\n\n**Modeling Twin Observations**\n\nI'm now modeling a Stag Hunt with twins who have different roles, receiving distinct observations. Each twin, observing their respective role, can choose between Stag and Hare. If both choose Stag, they receive a substantial reward, but there's a smaller reward for Hare and zero for Stag if the other chooses Hare. I am formalizing this test case now.\n\n\n\n**Formalizing Epistemic Impact**\n\nI'm formalizing the impact of Twin 1's action when using action-based FDT, and I now realize the impact of the prior $P$ on Twin 2's action is minimal. If Twin 1 intervenes and chooses \"Stag\", Twin 2 is unlikely to change her mind, and Twin 1 doesn't influence $\\textsc{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\textit{Role 2})$. The prior's weight ($q$) and the action-based intervention make cooperation unlikely, leading to suboptimal outcomes.\n\n\n**Modeling Coordination Failure**\n\nI'm now formalizing a division of labor scenario where each twin must select a frequency, with coordination required for payoff. Action-based FDT fails because one twin's action doesn't affect the other's, as their interventions are independent. The prior's weight ($q$) and the action-based intervention make cooperation unlikely, leading to suboptimal outcomes.\n\n\n**Refining the Twin Scenarios**\n\nI'm revisiting the asymmetric twin coordination problem, specifically when an agent is certain of their role and how the policy is implemented, contrasting it with action-based FDT. I'm focusing on the epistemic impact when one twin can only impact their action, which results in the failure of action-based FDT to produce cooperation.\n\n\n**Formalizing Subjunctive Dependencies**\n\nNow I'm formalizing the framework, focusing on the distinction between action-based and policy-based intervention, emphasizing subjunctive dependencies. I'm building on Footnotes 7 and 16, and contrasting EDT, CDT, standard FDT, and P-FDT using the primitives from the paper, like outcomes, actions, observations, and utilities. I'm focusing on the policy space to differentiate the two intervention types.\n\n\n**Formalizing the Framework**\n\nNow, I'm defining the framework using outcomes, actions, observations, and utilities from the paper, building on Footnotes 7 and 16. I'm focusing on the policy space to differentiate action-based and policy-based interventions, especially the graphical mechanics of P-FDT in contrast with other decision theories. I'm building on the distinction between action-based and policy-based intervention.\n\n\n**Elaborating on Policy Selection**\n\nI'm expanding my analysis to incorporate Kleene's second recursion theorem and its relation to counterpossible interventions, treating logical variables as stand-ins for $\\mathtt{true}(\\phi)$. I'm also planning to work through examples like Transparent Newcomb, Mechanical Blackmail, and Parfit's Hitchhiker, and show how P-FDT handles policy selection within these. The aim is to illustrate P-FDT's evaluation of single-agent dilemmas, highlighting updatelessness and subjunctive dependence.\n\n\n**Calculating Expected Utility**\n\nI'm now calculating exact expected utilities for Transparent Newcomb using P-FDT to demonstrate its superiority over action-based FDT. Specifically, I'm showing that $\\pi_{1,2}$ (one-box if full, two-box if empty) uniquely maximizes expected utility at \\$990,010, beating alternatives. This highlights the subtle mathematical advantage of policy selection even in single-agent scenarios.\n\n\n**Contrasting Newcomb Scenarios**\n\nNow I'm focusing on contrasting Transparent Newcomb under action-based and P-FDT frameworks. I can see that in action-based FDT, the prior parameter, $q$, affects the evaluation of the \"full\" box decision, as the two decision branches are independent. In P-FDT, this epistemic parameter is completely eliminated, as the policy sets the actions across all observations. I'm now working to formalize how this difference affects utility maximization in single-agent scenarios. I will also incorporate clear examples in Section 4 to distinguish the paper's claim.\n\n\n**Formalizing Decision Failures**\n\nNow I'm formalizing the Asymmetric Twin Dilemma, where action-based FDT fails because interventions are on independent branches.  P-FDT, however, simultaneously adjusts actions across both agents, leading to cooperation. This highlights why Equation 4 leads to suboptimal outcomes due to disjoint observations in multi-agent scenarios. The framework for policy selection is now clearer.\n\n\n**Distinguishing Policy Domains**\n\nI'm now clarifying the domain of the policy function. I'm focusing on whether a policy is a mapping from observations to actions ($\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$) or requires an additional component to the input ($\\Pi: \\mathcal{M} \\times \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$). I'm using the Smoking Lesion problem and multi-agent dilemmas to contrast observation policies with meta-policies. I'm also considering self-locating beliefs, and utility aggregation.\n\n\n**Clarifying Payoff Aggregation**\n\nI'm now focusing on the implications of payoff aggregation in multi-agent P-FDT scenarios, especially when agents have distinct observations and indexical identities. I'm exploring how the prior $P$ factors into utility evaluation before observations are made. I'm clarifying how this approach, where indexical identity is encoded in the observation, enables coordination without requiring altruistic utility functions. I'm also planning to clarify how to resolve dependencies.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Global Perspectives**\n\nI'm now focusing on a succinct conclusion for the essay. I see that P-FDT provides the \"global perspective\" that FDT requires. The essay's conclusion will emphasize P-FDT's fulfillment of FDT's core goal. I'll need to briefly summarize the advantages in a way that remains technically sound.\n\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":61184,"completion_tokens":23206,"total_tokens":84390,"cost":0.177214,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.177214,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.061184,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.11603},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":10475,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}